{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וירח", :translit "va-yarach", :gloss "and He smelled"} {:he "וירח את ריח בגדיו", :translit "va-yarach et reiach begadav", :gloss "and he smelled the fragrance of his garments"} {:he "אפי' פושעי ישראל עתידין שיתנו ריח", :translit "afilu poshei yisra'el atidin she-yitnu reiach", :gloss "even the transgressors of Israel are destined to give fragrance"} {:he "בגדיו", :translit "begadav", :gloss "his garments"} {:he "בוגדיו", :translit "bogadav", :gloss "his traitors"} {:he "וירח ה' את ריח הניחוח", :translit "va-yarach HaShem et reiach ha-nichoach", :gloss "and the LORD smelled the fragrance of the sweet savor"} {:he "ניחוח", :translit "nichoach", :gloss "sweet savor"} {:he "ריח ג\"ע", :translit "reiach gan eden", :gloss "fragrance of Garden of Eden"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וירח" "וירח את ריח בגדיו"]} {:kind :other, :note "Al tikri substitution: בגדיו read as בוגדיו (his garments → his traitors)"}], :frame {:text "The tradition that even the transgressors of Israel are destined to give off fragrance is found in the Talmud (Sanhedrin 111a), which expounds the verse in Song of Songs 4:11 and connects it to the reading of בגדיו as בוגדיו. The phrase וירח ה' את ריח הניחוח appears in Leviticus 1:9 and throughout the sacrificial passages. The idea that the fragrance of the Garden of Eden is connected to garments draws on the tradition that the garments God made for Adam and Eve (Genesis 3:21) came from the Garden of Eden and retained its fragrance.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 111a" "Song of Songs 4:11" "Leviticus 1:9" "Genesis 3:21"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מנעריו", :translit "mi-ne'urav", :gloss "from his youth"} {:he "איש מלחמה מנעוריו", :translit "ish milchamah mi-ne'urav", :gloss "a man of war from his youth"} {:he "שאנן מואב מנעוריו", :translit "sha'anan mo'av mi-ne'urav", :gloss "at ease is Moav from his youth"} {:he "יצה\"ר", :translit "yetzer ha-ra", :gloss "evil inclination"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["מנעריו" "איש מלחמה מנעוריו" "שאנן מואב מנעוריו"]}], :frame {:text "The obligation to battle the evil inclination from youth is expressed in Ecclesiastes 11:10 (\"and remove anger from your heart and put away evil from your flesh, for youth and dawn are vanity\"). The Sages identify the yetzer ha-ra as emerging from the moment of birth and warring against a person all his days, as stated in the Talmud (Sukkah 52b) and Bereshit Rabbah (22:6). The image of the righteous person at rest upon his watch draws on the language of Isaiah 7:4 (\"be quiet and at rest\") regarding Moav's complacency, here repurposed as the reward of one who has conquered his inclination.", :sources ["Ecclesiastes 11:10" "Sukkah 52b" "Bereshit Rabbah 22:6" "Isaiah 7:4"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וירח" "וירח את ריח בגדיו"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "וירח", :refs ["Genesis 8:21" "Genesis 27:27" "Joshua 10:12" "Joshua 10:13" "Isaiah 13:10" "Ezekiel 32:7" "Joel 2:10" "Joel 4:15"], :n 11, :lens :exact} {:phrase "וירח את ריח בגדיו", :refs ["Genesis 27:27"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Al tikri substitution: בגדיו read as בוגדיו (his garments → his traitors)", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["מנעריו" "איש מלחמה מנעוריו" "שאנן מואב מנעוריו"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "מנעריו", :refs ["Genesis 8:21" "1 Samuel 17:33"], :n 2, :lens :exact} {:phrase "איש מלחמה מנעוריו", :refs ["1 Samuel 17:33"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "שאנן מואב מנעוריו", :refs ["Jeremiah 48:11"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:05:19.195159975Z", :model "glm-5.2"}